Let $\mathbf{Grp}$ denote the category of groups and $\mathbf{Ab}$ the full subcategory of abelian groups. Recall:

- $A: \mathbf{Grp} \to \mathbf{Ab}$ is the **Abelianizer** functor: on objects $A(G) = G/[G,G]$, and on morphisms $A(f: G \to H)$ is the unique homomorphism $\bar f: G/[G,G] \to H/[H,H]$ satisfying $\bar f \circ \pi_G = \pi_H \circ f$.
- $I: \mathbf{Ab} \to \mathbf{Grp}$ is the **inclusion** functor: on objects it regards an abelian group as a group, and on morphisms it regards an abelian-group homomorphism as a group homomorphism.
- Hence $IA = I \circ A: \mathbf{Grp} \to \mathbf{Grp}$ sends $G \mapsto G/[G,G]$ (viewed as a group) and $f \mapsto \bar f$ (viewed as a group homomorphism).
- $1_{\mathbf{Grp}}: \mathbf{Grp} \to \mathbf{Grp}$ is the identity functor.

For every group $G$ define the component
$$\pi_G := \pi_G^{\text{can}}: G \longrightarrow G/[G,G], \qquad g \longmapsto g\,[G,G],$$
the canonical quotient homomorphism. We claim this assembles into a natural transformation
$$\pi: 1_{\mathbf{Grp}} \Longrightarrow IA.$$

**Well-definedness of each component.** $[G,G] \trianglelefteq G$ is normal (commutator subgroups are characteristic, hence normal), so the quotient $G/[G,G]$ is a group and $\pi_G$ is a surjective group homomorphism. Moreover $G/[G,G]$ is abelian (since $[g_1[G,G], g_2[G,G]] = [g_1,g_2][G,G] = [G,G]$, the identity), so $G/[G,G] \in \mathbf{Ab}$ and $\pi_G$ is a group homomorphism $G \to I(A(G))$ as required. Thus $\pi_G: 1_{\mathbf{Grp}}(G) = G \to IA(G) = G/[G,G]$ is a well-defined arrow of $\mathbf{Grp}$.

**Naturality.** We must show that for every group homomorphism $f: G \to H$ the square

$$\require{AMScd}
\begin{CD}
G @>{\pi_G}>> IA(G) = G/[G,G]\\
@V{f}VV @VV{IA(f)}V\\
H @>>{\pi_H}> IA(H) = H/[H,H]
\end{CD}$$

commutes, i.e. $\pi_H \circ f = IA(f) \circ \pi_G$.

*Step 1 — $A(f)$ is well defined.* For any commutator $[x,y] \in [G,G]$,
$$f([x,y]) = f(xyx^{-1}y^{-1}) = f(x)f(y)f(x)^{-1}f(y)^{-1} = [f(x),f(y)] \in [H,H].$$
Since $[G,G]$ is generated by commutators and $f$ is a homomorphism, $f([G,G]) \subseteq [H,H]$. By the universal property of the quotient, there is a **unique** homomorphism $\bar f: G/[G,G] \to H/[H,H]$ with $\bar f \circ \pi_G = \pi_H \circ f$. This $\bar f$ is precisely $A(f)$, and $IA(f) = I(\bar f) = \bar f$ (the same map, regarded as a group homomorphism via inclusion).

*Step 2 — the square commutes.* Unwinding the definitions,
$$(IA(f) \circ \pi_G)(g) = \bar f(g\,[G,G]) = \bar f(\pi_G(g)) = (\pi_H \circ f)(g) = f(g)\,[H,H],$$
for every $g \in G$. Hence $\pi_H \circ f = IA(f) \circ \pi_G$, which is exactly the commutativity of the naturality square.

**Functoriality consistency (sanity check).** Because $A$ is a functor, $A(1_G) = 1_{A(G)}$ and $A(f \circ g) = A(f) \circ A(g)$; inclusion $I$ preserves these, so $IA$ is a genuine functor $\mathbf{Grp} \to \mathbf{Grp}$. The construction above assigns to every object $G$ an arrow $\pi_G: G \to IA(G)$ and to every morphism $f: G \to H$ a commuting square, which is precisely the definition of a natural transformation $1_{\mathbf{Grp}} \Rightarrow IA$.

**Conclusion.** The family $\{\pi_G: G \to G/[G,G]\}_{G \in \mathbf{Grp}}$ is a natural transformation $\pi: 1_{\mathbf{Grp}} \Longrightarrow IA$, because each $\pi_G$ is a well-defined group homomorphism $1_{\mathbf{Grp}}(G) \to IA(G)$, and for every homomorphism $f: G \to H$ the relation $\pi_H \circ f = IA(f) \circ \pi_G$ holds — the latter being exactly the defining equation $\bar f \circ \pi_G = \pi_H \circ f$ of the induced map on abelianizations. $\blacksquare$

